In article <1nolpbINNo56@life.ai.mit.edu> rassilon@hal.gnu.ai.mit.edu (Brian Preble) writes:

   First off, I'd like to compliment the Dev Team on the addition of grease.
   This item provides a marvelous way of temporarily rustproofing an item.
   I would like to make a couple of comments and suggestions regarding it's
   use, however.

So *that's* what grease is for!  I had been wondering... :^)

   o  If grease is applied to a Polished Shield, Mirror, etc. the object
      still reflects perfectly (or at least well enough to stone a Medusa).
      This seems unrealistic.  Perhaps a greased reflective item could be
      made nonreflective while greased?

A *thin* coating might continue to work, though; or oil would work.  (Hey,
maybe you could drain a few charges' worth of oil from your lamp and rub it
on the metal?)

   o  There is no reliable way to remove grease from an item.  I realize
      that at this stage of the game this isn't useful, but if the above
      behavior were implemented then a way to remove the grease is needed.
      I suggest #apply'ing a towel or dipping the item in water.  As this
      would logically dirty the towel, perhaps #dipping the towel in water
      would cleanse it.  i.e.,

       #apply towel to greased Shield of Reflection
       You polish the Shield to its original state.
       #dip greased towel in water
       You wash the grease off the towel.

   Comments?
Ever manage to get grease off of something by dipping it in water?  It's
never worked for me without soap as well.  Then again, oil would float off
with a little encouragement...  But definitely, the towel should work.
Good thought!

